# Agent guide for {{pluginName}}

A [Nextly](https://nextlyhq.com) plugin. This is a publishable **library**, not
an app: `src/` is what ships, and `dev/` is a throwaway Nextly app that exists
only so you can exercise the plugin in a real admin.

## Where things are

| path                    | what it is                                                       |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `src/index.ts`          | the public entry — React-free, so it stays importable on the server |
| `src/admin/index.ts`    | the `./admin` subpath, where React components are registered      |
| `src/plugin.ts`         | the plugin factory: what it contributes and what it does on init  |
| `src/collections/`      | collections the plugin contributes to its host                    |
| `dev/`                  | the embedded playground: a minimal Nextly app on SQLite           |
| `dist/`                 | the build output, and the only thing `npm publish` ships          |

## Commands

```bash
{{runCommand}} dev            # the embedded playground → http://localhost:3000/admin
{{runCommand}} test           # vitest, against a real Nextly on in-memory SQLite
{{runCommand}} build          # tsup → dist/
{{runCommand}} check-types    # tsc --noEmit
{{runCommand}} lint           # eslint
```

`{{runCommand}} dev` runs `next dev dev` — Next.js with `dev/` as the project root.
Editing anything under `src/` reloads the playground, so the loop is the same as
working on an app.

## Things that surprise people

- **Plugin APIs come from `@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk`; schema APIs come from
  `nextly/config`.** The SDK is the stability boundary for everything a plugin
  does AS a plugin: `definePlugin`, `registerComponents` and
  `registerKnownPlugin` from `@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk/admin`, `createTestNextly`
  from `@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk/testing`, field factories, `pluginField`,
  `NextlyError`. Collections and singles are defined the same way an app defines
  them, with `defineCollection` from `nextly/config` — which is what
  `src/collections/example.ts` does.

  **What is never supported is importing `@nextlyhq/admin` directly.** It carries
  no compatibility promise to plugin authors, so an import that compiles today
  breaks on a patch release. If you need something from it that the SDK does not
  re-export, that is worth raising rather than reaching past the boundary.

- **The root export must stay React-free.** It is imported by the host's server
  config, so a React import at `src/index.ts` breaks apps that never render your
  admin UI. Components belong on the `./admin` subpath, which is where the host
  looks for them.
- **Resolve your own slugs through `ctx.self`.** A host can rename a collection
  your plugin contributes; `ctx.self.collections.examples` follows the rename,
  a hardcoded `"examples"` does not.
- **`dev/` is never published.** `files` is `["dist"]`, so the playground can
  depend on anything it likes without adding weight for consumers.
- **Peers, not dependencies.** `nextly`, `@nextlyhq/admin`,
  `@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk` and React are peer dependencies — the host supplies
  them, and bundling a second copy is what causes the "two Reacts" class of
  failure.

## Testing

`createTestNextly` from `@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk/testing` boots a real Nextly on
in-memory SQLite with your plugin registered, so a test exercises the same
lifecycle a host would run:

```ts
const t = await createTestNextly({ plugins: [myPlugin()] });
```

**Pass `plugins` alone.** Registration auto-syncs the tables for contributed
collections, so passing the same collections again as `collections` registers
those slugs a second time as code-owned entities and boot fails with
`NEXTLY_SCHEMA_SLUG_COLLISION`.

Assert on behaviour through `t.nextly` rather than on the plugin object: what a
host observes is the contract, and the object shape is not.

## Which Nextly this is, and where its docs are

An agent working here will otherwise answer from whatever it remembers of some
other version. Ask the project instead:

```bash
{{execCommand}} nextly --version      # the running core version
```

That is the same version the plugin resolver validates a plugin's `nextly`
range against, so a compatibility answer and a reported version cannot
disagree. The dependency range this project pins is in `package.json` under
`nextly`; the resolved version is what the command above prints.

**The authoritative documentation is <https://nextlyhq.com/docs>.** Two files
are published for machine readers and are the ones to fetch:

| file | what it is |
| ---- | ---------- |
| <https://nextlyhq.com/llms.txt> | the index — every documentation page, with a one-line summary |
| <https://nextlyhq.com/llms-full.txt> | the same documentation as one file |

Both are generated from the pages the site publishes, so they cannot describe a
page that does not exist. Neither is version-stamped: they describe the CURRENT
release, so check the version above before trusting a detail against an older
pin.

## Conventions worth keeping

- Version `nextly` compatibility in the factory's `nextly` range, and widen it
  deliberately rather than by default.
- Clean up in `destroy()`. Event subscriptions are dropped for you on registry
  reset; timers, watchers and connections are not.
- Keep `contributes` declarative. Work belongs in `init()`, where the context is
  available and failures are attributable to your plugin.

